/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/fortinet-fortigate.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("fortinet-fortigate", [["fg1",0,1,"A new FortiGate appliance is unboxed and an admin cables a laptop to the MGMT port. Which address should they browse to for the first GUI login?",["https://192.168.1.99","https://192.168.100.99:10443","https://10.0.0.1","https://172.16.1.1"],0,"Hardware FortiGates ship with 192.168.1.99 on the management (or internal/port1) interface. The other addresses are common defaults on other vendors' gear, not FortiGate.","Initial configuration",[null,"192.168.100.99:10443 is a common management address on other vendors' gear (and FortiManager/analyzer style ports), not a factory FortiGate default.","10.0.0.1 is a generic gateway address, not what a factory FortiGate ships with on its management interface.","172.16.1.1 is another arbitrary private-range gateway, not the FortiGate default of 192.168.1.99."]],["fg2",0,1,"What happens the first time an admin logs in to a factory-default FortiGate appliance?",["They log in as admin with the password fortinet and are then asked to register","They log in as root with the unit's serial number","They must first register the unit in FortiCloud","They log in as admin with a blank password and must set one"],3,"The default account is admin with an empty password, and FortiOS forces a new password at first login. There is no root account, and FortiCloud registration is optional for local login.","Initial configuration",["The default password is blank, not 'fortinet', and registration is not required just to log in locally.","FortiOS has no root account and never logs you in with the serial number; the default account is admin.","FortiCloud registration is optional for local login; you can log in and manage the unit without it.",null]],["fg3",0,1,"Security policy says administrators may only log in to the FortiGate from the 10.10.99.0/24 management subnet. Which administrator setting enforces this most directly?",["A stricter admin password policy","A custom administrator profile limited to the management VDOM","Trusted hosts on each administrator account","Two-factor authentication for admins"],2,"Trusted hosts limit the source addresses an admin account can log in from. Profiles control what an admin may change, not where they log in from, and MFA or password rules do not restrict source networks.","Initial configuration",["A password policy strengthens credentials but does nothing to restrict which source subnet an admin may connect from.","An admin profile controls what an admin can change, and VDOM scope limits which VDOM they manage, not the source IP they log in from.",null,"MFA adds a second factor but still allows login from any network; it does not limit the source subnet."]],["fg4",0,1,"An auditor needs to view logs and reports on the FortiGate but must not change any settings. What should the admin create?",["A super_admin account restricted with trusted hosts on the audit subnet","An admin profile with read-only log and report access","A local firewall user in a user group","A new VDOM holding only log settings"],1,"Admin profiles set read or read-write access per area, so a read-only profile for Log & Report fits. super_admin can change everything, and firewall users cannot log in to administration at all.","Initial configuration",["super_admin has full read-write access, so even with trusted hosts the auditor could change settings, which violates the requirement.",null,"A local firewall user authenticates to policies for traffic; it cannot log in to the administrative GUI at all.","A VDOM separates configuration domains; it does not create a read-only auditor login and is far more than is needed."]],["fg5",0,1,"In an FGCP cluster with HA override disabled (the default), which criterion is compared first when electing the primary?",["Device priority","Number of connected monitored interfaces","HA uptime","The serial number, highest value winning"],1,"With override disabled, FGCP compares monitored interfaces, then HA uptime, then priority, then serial number. Priority is only checked before uptime when override is enabled.","Configure an FGCP HA cluster",["Device priority is only checked before uptime when HA override is enabled; with override disabled it comes after uptime.",null,"HA uptime is compared second, after the number of connected monitored interfaces, not first.","The serial number is only the final tie-breaker, checked last when everything else is equal."]],["fg6",0,1,"An admin raises the HA priority of the secondary FortiGate to 250, but it stays secondary. Override is disabled and both units have all monitored interfaces up. Why?",["HA uptime is compared before priority while override is disabled","Priority only applies after the cluster is rebooted twice","A priority above 200 is ignored by FGCP","The secondary's serial number is lower than the primary's"],0,"With override disabled, the unit with the higher HA uptime (beyond the uptime margin) stays primary regardless of priority. Enabling override, or resetting uptime, makes priority take effect.","Configure an FGCP HA cluster",[null,"Priority is a real election factor once override is enabled; it is not gated behind two reboots.","FGCP does not cap or ignore priority values above 200; 250 is a valid priority.","Serial number is only the last tie-breaker; here uptime already decides before serial is ever considered."]],["fg7",0,1,"After an HA failover, users complain that long file transfers and SSH sessions dropped. What should the admin enable so TCP sessions survive the next failover?",["HA override on both cluster members","A second dedicated heartbeat interface","Uninterruptible firmware upgrade","Session pickup in the HA settings"],3,"Session pickup synchronizes the session table so the new primary can continue existing TCP sessions; it is disabled by default. Override and extra heartbeat links affect election and resilience, not session continuity.","Configure an FGCP HA cluster",["HA override changes which unit becomes primary; it does nothing to preserve existing TCP sessions across a failover.","A second heartbeat link improves cluster resilience and split-brain avoidance, but it does not carry the session table for continuity.","Uninterruptible firmware upgrade keeps traffic flowing during an upgrade; it does not sync sessions for an unplanned failover.",null]],["fg8",0,1,"Two HA members show as out of sync in the GUI. Which command compares configuration checksums across the cluster members?",["get system performance status","diagnose sys session list","diagnose sys ha checksum cluster","diagnose debug flow trace start 10"],2,"diagnose sys ha checksum cluster shows the checksum of each configuration area on every member so you can find what differs. The others show resources, sessions and packet flow.","Configure an FGCP HA cluster",["get system performance status shows CPU, memory and sessions, not per-area configuration checksums.","diagnose sys session list dumps the live session table, which has nothing to do with config sync between members.",null,"That command traces packet flow for troubleshooting connectivity, not HA configuration checksums."]],["fg9",0,1,"Which settings must match for two FortiGates to form an FGCP cluster?",["Hostname, management IP address and the admin password","Serial number prefix and license expiry dates","Model, firmware, HA group ID, group name and password","Interface IP addresses on every port"],2,"FGCP requires the same hardware model and firmware and matching HA group ID, group name and password. Hostnames and management IPs are unique per member.","Configure an FGCP HA cluster",["Hostnames and management IPs are unique per member, and the admin password is not an FGCP cluster-matching requirement.","A shared serial prefix or matching license dates are not FGCP formation requirements; members can differ there.",null,"Interface IPs are not matched across members; each unit keeps its own addressing while sharing virtual IP/MAC."]],["fg10",0,1,"How does FGCP active-active mode differ from active-passive mode?",["Every member owns its own set of interface IP addresses","The primary shares security-profile inspection sessions with secondaries","The secondaries route traffic with no connection to the primary","Session synchronization is turned off by design"],1,"In active-active, the primary still receives traffic but distributes sessions that need inspection to the secondaries. In active-passive only the primary processes traffic. The cluster shares virtual IP and MAC addresses in both modes.","Configure an FGCP HA cluster",["Both modes share virtual IP and MAC addresses; members do not each own separate interface IPs in active-active.",null,"Secondaries always coordinate with the primary; they never route independently with no connection to it.","Session synchronization is not turned off in active-active; the primary distributes inspection sessions to secondaries."]],["fg11",0,1,"A branch FortiGate must join the headquarters Security Fabric, where HQ is the root. What is the correct approach?",["Enable Fabric on the branch, point it at the root and authorize it there","Import the root's configuration backup onto the branch","Build an IPsec tunnel and enable fabric-sync on it","Register both units under the same FortiCare account only"],0,"A downstream FortiGate enables the Security Fabric connection, uses the upstream (root) IP, and the root authorizes it by serial number. Sharing a FortiCare account or a tunnel does not by itself create the Fabric.","Implement the Fortinet Security Fabric",[null,"Importing the root's config backup would overwrite the branch's own settings and does not create a Fabric authorization.","A Fabric connection is not established by enabling sync on an IPsec tunnel; the downstream unit points at the upstream and is authorized.","Sharing a FortiCare account registers the units but does not by itself join them into a Security Fabric."]],["fg12",0,1,"What must the root FortiGate have before the Security Fabric can be set up?",["A FortiManager managing every downstream unit","At least one FortiSwitch connected over FortiLink","A dedicated HA heartbeat link to each downstream unit","Logging to FortiAnalyzer or a cloud logging service"],3,"The Fabric root needs FortiAnalyzer or cloud logging (such as FortiGate Cloud) so Fabric logs and views can be built. FortiManager and FortiSwitch are optional Fabric devices.","Implement the Fortinet Security Fabric",["FortiManager centralizes configuration but is an optional Fabric device, not a prerequisite for the root.","A FortiSwitch over FortiLink is an optional Fabric member, not required for the Fabric to be set up.","HA heartbeat links are for clustering between paired units, not a requirement for downstream Fabric members.",null]],["fg13",0,1,"The security team wants an email every time any administrator changes the FortiGate configuration. What is the simplest way to do this?",["A syslog server with a filter that forwards matching events to a shared mailbox","An event log report scheduled once per day","A FortiGuard outbreak alert subscription","An automation stitch with a configuration-change trigger and an email action"],3,"Automation stitches pair a trigger (such as a configuration change) with actions (such as email). A daily report is not immediate, and syslog forwarding needs extra tools.","Implement the Fortinet Security Fabric",["A syslog server with a mailbox filter needs extra external tooling and is more complex than a built-in stitch.","A daily scheduled report is not immediate, so admins would not get an email at the moment of each change.","FortiGuard outbreak alerts are threat-intelligence notifications, not alerts about local configuration changes.",null]],["fg14",0,1,"What does the Security Rating feature provide?",["A FortiGuard reputation score for each external website","Live bandwidth ratings for each WAN link","Best-practice checks across Fabric devices with a score and fixes","A per-user risk score based on failed logins"],2,"Security Rating runs checks against Fortinet best practices on Fabric devices and shows a score with recommendations. It does not rate websites or WAN links.","Implement the Fortinet Security Fabric",["Website reputation scores come from FortiGuard web filtering, not from the Security Rating feature.","Live WAN bandwidth ratings are shown by SD-WAN/monitoring views, not by Security Rating.",null,"Per-user failed-login risk is not what Security Rating produces; it audits device best practices."]],["fg15",0,1,"A FortiGate enters memory conserve mode during a traffic spike. Which global setting decides whether sessions needing proxy-based antivirus pass uninspected or are blocked?",["ips fail-open","av-failopen","tcp-halfopen-timer","admin-lockout-threshold"],1,"The av-failopen setting under system global controls what proxy inspection does in conserve mode. IPS fail-open controls the IPS engine, and the others are timers or lockout settings.","Diagnose resource and connectivity problems",["ips fail-open governs the IPS engine when it is overloaded, not proxy-based antivirus behavior during conserve mode.",null,"tcp-halfopen-timer controls how long half-open TCP sessions live; it is unrelated to conserve-mode inspection.","admin-lockout-threshold limits failed admin logins; it has nothing to do with proxy AV under memory pressure."]],["fg16",0,1,"Which command gives a quick summary of CPU use, memory use, session counts and uptime on a FortiGate?",["get system performance status","get system status","get router info routing-table all","diagnose sys ha status"],0,"get system performance status summarizes CPU, memory, sessions and uptime. get system status shows firmware, serial and mode, not live resources.","Diagnose resource and connectivity problems",[null,"get system status shows firmware, serial number and operating mode, not live CPU, memory or session counts.","That command prints the routing table, not resource utilization.","diagnose sys ha status reports HA cluster state, not the unit's CPU/memory/session summary."]],["fg17",0,1,"A debug flow for a failing connection shows \"Denied by forward policy check (policy 0)\". What does this tell the admin?",["No policy matched, so the implicit deny dropped the traffic","Policy ID 0 is a user policy waiting for authentication","The route lookup failed before any firewall policy was checked","An IPS sensor on the first policy blocked the session"],0,"Policy 0 is the implicit deny at the bottom of the policy list, so no configured policy matched. A routing failure or IPS block shows a different debug message.","Diagnose resource and connectivity problems",[null,"There is no user policy 0; policy 0 is the implicit deny, and pending authentication produces a different flow message.","A route lookup failure shows a 'no route' message; here a policy was checked and matched the implicit deny.","An IPS block logs an IPS signature action, not a 'Denied by forward policy check (policy 0)' line."]],["fg18",0,1,"An admin runs diagnose debug flow filter addr 10.1.1.5 and diagnose debug flow trace start 20, but no output appears while the host sends traffic. What was missed?",["diagnose debug reset","execute ping-options source 10.1.1.5","diagnose sys session clear","diagnose debug enable"],3,"Debug output is only printed to the console after diagnose debug enable. Resetting debug clears filters, and clearing sessions would disrupt users without showing anything.","Diagnose resource and connectivity problems",["diagnose debug reset clears filters; running it would remove the filter you just set, not produce output.","Setting a ping source address affects locally generated pings, not whether debug flow prints for transit traffic.","Clearing sessions would disrupt users and still not print any debug output on its own.",null]],["fg19",0,1,"A policy logs only security events, but the analyst needs a record of every accepted session through it. What should be changed?",["Enable log violation traffic on policy 0","Raise the event log severity to debug","Set Log allowed traffic to All sessions","Enable local disk logging for event logs"],2,"All sessions logs every accepted session; Security events only logs sessions a security profile acted on. Implicit-deny logging records denied traffic, not allowed sessions.","Deployment and system configuration: logging",["Policy 0 is the implicit deny; its logging records denied traffic, not accepted sessions through a real policy.","Event log severity controls system/event logging verbosity, not per-policy logging of allowed traffic.",null,"Enabling disk logging changes where logs are stored, not whether the policy records every allowed session."]],["fg20",0,1,"A small FortiGate without a disk logs to memory. After a reboot for a firmware upgrade, yesterday's logs are gone. What should the admin do?",["Increase the memory log size to the maximum the model allows","Send logs to FortiAnalyzer, FortiGate Cloud or syslog","Lower the log severity from information to warning","Enable log compression on the memory buffer"],1,"Memory logs are small and lost on reboot, so keeping history needs a remote log destination. A bigger or compressed memory buffer is still cleared on restart.","Deployment and system configuration: logging",["A larger memory buffer still lives in RAM and is wiped on reboot, so yesterday's logs would still be lost.",null,"Lowering severity only reduces how much is logged; it does nothing to make memory logs survive a restart.","Compression saves space in the same volatile buffer; the logs are still cleared when the unit restarts."]],["fg21",0,1,"What is a virtual domain (VDOM) on a FortiGate?",["A DNS zone that the FortiGate hosts and answers for internal clients","A separate instance with its own policies, routes and settings","A group of admins who share one administrator profile","A VLAN that carries HA heartbeat traffic"],1,"Each VDOM acts like an independent FortiGate with its own interfaces, routing and policies. DNS zones, admin groups and VLANs are unrelated features.","Initial configuration",["A DNS zone is a name-resolution construct; a VDOM is a full virtual firewall instance, not a hosted zone.",null,"A VDOM is not a group of administrators; admin permissions are set by admin profiles.","A VDOM is not a VLAN, and HA heartbeat traffic uses dedicated heartbeat interfaces, not a VDOM."]],["fg22",0,1,"A FortiGate runs an old 7.0 build and must be moved to FortiOS 7.6. What should the admin do first?",["Back up the config and follow the published upgrade path","Install the 7.6 image directly and reboot twice","Factory reset, then restore the 7.0 configuration file onto 7.6","Upgrade the secondary HA member only and wait"],0,"Fortinet publishes an upgrade path that steps through intermediate builds so the configuration converts cleanly, and a backup allows rollback. Skipping steps can lose settings.","Initial configuration",[null,"Installing 7.6 directly and rebooting can skip required intermediate builds, risking a broken config conversion.","Restoring a 7.0 config file onto 7.6 bypasses the guided upgrade conversion and can lose or corrupt settings.","Upgrading only the secondary HA member leaves a mixed-firmware cluster and does not complete the migration path."]],["fg23",0,2,"How does a FortiGate choose which firewall policy applies to a new session?",["It uses the most specific matching policy anywhere in the list","It uses the policy with the lowest policy ID","It combines every policy that matches the session","It checks policies top-down and uses the first match"],3,"Policies are evaluated in list order and the first match wins. Policy ID is only a label; order in the list decides.","Configure firewall policies",["FortiGate does not pick the 'most specific' policy anywhere in the list; order in the list decides.","Lowest policy ID is not the selector; ID is just a label and does not set evaluation order.","Policies are not combined; exactly one policy (the first match) applies to the session.",null]],["fg24",0,2,"An admin adds a policy allowing HR to reach a payroll server, but HR still cannot connect. A broader deny policy for the whole LAN sits above it. What is the fix?",["Give the HR policy a lower policy ID number","Enable NAT on the HR policy","Move the HR policy above the broader deny policy","Add the payroll server to the deny policy's destination"],2,"Because the first matching policy wins, the specific allow must sit above the broad deny. Changing the ID does not change order, and NAT does not affect matching.","Configure firewall policies",["Changing the policy ID does not change list order, so the broad deny above would still match first.","Enabling NAT affects address translation, not which policy matches, so HR would still be denied.",null,"Adding the payroll server to the deny policy's destination would block it further, the opposite of the goal."]],["fg25",0,2,"Denied connections are not appearing in the forward traffic log. What should the admin enable?",["Log allowed traffic set to All sessions on every accept policy","A higher event log severity level","Logging of violation traffic on the implicit deny policy","Packet capture on the WAN interface"],2,"The implicit deny policy does not log by default. Turning on its logging records the sessions it blocks; allowed-traffic logging covers accepted sessions only.","Configure firewall policies",["Allowed-traffic logging records accepted sessions; it does not capture the connections the implicit deny blocks.","Event log severity governs system events, not logging of denied forward traffic.",null,"A WAN packet capture is a manual diagnostic, not a way to log denied connections in the forward traffic log."]],["fg26",0,2,"Which of these is not used when matching traffic to a firewall policy?",["The incoming and outgoing interfaces","The security profiles attached to it","The source and destination addresses","The service and schedule"],1,"FortiGate matches on interfaces, source (address or user), destination, service and schedule. Security profiles are applied after a match; they do not decide it.","Configure firewall policies",["Interfaces are part of policy matching, so they are used, not excluded.",null,"Source and destination addresses are core matching criteria, so they are used in matching.","Service and schedule are matching criteria as well, so they are used, not excluded."]],["fg27",0,2,"Internal users need Internet access, and the FortiGate has one public IP on its WAN port. Which source NAT choice is simplest?",["Enable NAT with the outgoing interface address","Create a VIP for each internal host","Enable central SNAT with a one-to-one IP pool of public addresses","Create a port-forwarding VIP on the WAN IP"],0,"Using the outgoing interface address hides all internal hosts behind the WAN IP with port translation. VIPs are for destination NAT, and one-to-one pools need one public IP per host.","Configure SNAT and DNAT options",[null,"A VIP per host is destination NAT for inbound services, not a way to give outbound users Internet access.","Central SNAT with a one-to-one pool needs a public IP per host and is far more complex than needed for one WAN IP.","A port-forwarding VIP publishes inbound services; it does not provide outbound source NAT for internal users."]],["fg28",0,2,"A company owns four public IPs and wants 800 internal users to share them for outbound traffic. Which IP pool type fits?",["One-to-one","Fixed port range","Port block allocation per VIP","Overload"],3,"Overload translates many internal addresses to the pool's addresses with port translation. One-to-one gives each user a whole public IP, so only four users could be online.","Configure SNAT and DNAT options",["One-to-one gives each user a whole public IP, so only four users could be online at once with four IPs.","Fixed port range assigns predictable external port blocks but is not the simplest fit for mass outbound sharing here.","Port block allocation is tied to VIP/CGN-style setups and is not the standard many-to-few outbound overload choice.",null]],["fg29",0,2,"What is a limit of a one-to-one IP pool?",["It can only be used with IPsec tunnels","It randomly changes source ports on every packet it translates","It needs central SNAT to be enabled","Only as many hosts as pool addresses can connect at once"],3,"One-to-one maps each internal host to its own external address without port translation, so the pool size caps concurrent users. It works with normal policies.","Configure SNAT and DNAT options",["One-to-one pools work with normal IPv4 policies, not only with IPsec tunnels.","One-to-one does not translate ports at all, let alone randomize them on every packet.","One-to-one is configured directly on a policy; it does not require central SNAT to be enabled.",null]],["fg30",0,2,"A web server at 10.0.1.10 must be reachable from the Internet on public IP 203.0.113.20. What should the admin configure?",["An overload IP pool on the WAN-to-LAN policy","A static route to 10.0.1.10 through the WAN interface","A VIP mapping 203.0.113.20 to 10.0.1.10 used in a WAN-to-LAN policy","Central SNAT with 10.0.1.10 as the translated source"],2,"Destination NAT is done with a VIP object, which is referenced as the destination of an inbound policy. IP pools and SNAT change the source, not the destination.","Configure SNAT and DNAT options",["An overload IP pool changes the source of outbound traffic; it does not publish an internal server inbound.","A static route affects path selection, not destination NAT; the server still would not be reachable on the public IP.",null,"Central SNAT translates the source address; reaching an internal server from outside needs destination NAT via a VIP."]],["fg31",0,2,"External users must reach an internal app on public port 8443, but the server listens on 443. How should the VIP be set up?",["Create two VIPs, one for each port, with the same IP","Enable port forwarding and map external 8443 to mapped 443","Set the policy service to 443 and leave the VIP static","Add a custom service that rewrites 8443 to 443"],1,"Port forwarding in a VIP translates both the address and the port. Services only match traffic and never rewrite ports.","Configure SNAT and DNAT options",["Two VIPs on the same IP for one service is unnecessary; a single VIP with port forwarding handles the 8443-to-443 mapping.",null,"Setting the policy service to 443 does not translate the external 8443 to the internal 443; services never rewrite ports.","A custom service only matches traffic on given ports; it cannot rewrite 8443 to 443, which is a VIP function."]],["fg32",0,2,"What changes when central SNAT is enabled on a FortiGate?",["SNAT moves out of policies into a separate top-down SNAT table","Every policy automatically uses the WAN IP as source","VIPs stop working until they are recreated","Destination NAT is also moved into the individual firewall policies"],0,"With central SNAT, source NAT rules are defined in their own ordered table instead of per policy. Destination NAT still uses VIPs (DNAT and VIP table).","Configure SNAT and DNAT options",[null,"Central SNAT does not force every policy to use the WAN IP; you define explicit SNAT rules in the table.","VIPs continue to work under central SNAT; destination NAT still uses the VIP table and does not need recreating.","Destination NAT is not moved into policies; it stays in the VIP/DNAT table while only SNAT becomes central."]],["fg33",0,2,"Users need access to Microsoft 365, and the admin does not want to maintain its long, changing list of IPs. What should the policy use as destination?",["An Internet Service Database (ISDB) entry for Microsoft 365","An FQDN address object for www.microsoft.com and login.microsoftonline.com","A geography address object for the United States","A wildcard FQDN for *.com"],0,"ISDB objects are kept up to date by FortiGuard with the service's addresses and ports. One FQDN misses many endpoints, and geography or wildcard objects are far too broad.","Configure firewall policies",[null,"A couple of FQDN objects miss the many changing endpoints Microsoft 365 uses, so access would be incomplete.","A US geography object is far too broad, allowing all US-hosted sites rather than just Microsoft 365.","A wildcard *.com is essentially the whole Internet and provides no meaningful restriction."]],["fg34",0,2,"Gaming sites should only be allowed from 12:00 to 13:00 on weekdays. What should be attached to the allow policy?",["A one-time schedule for today at 12:00","A web filter warning action on the gaming category","A traffic shaper limited at noon","A recurring schedule for weekdays 12:00–13:00"],3,"Recurring schedules repeat on chosen days and times. One-time schedules run once, and shapers limit bandwidth rather than time.","Configure firewall policies",["A one-time schedule runs only once for today; it will not repeat the noon window on every weekday.","A web filter warning action shows a click-through page; it does not restrict access to a specific time window.","A traffic shaper limits bandwidth, not the hours during which the sites are allowed.",null]],["fg35",0,2,"A policy requires users to be in the Staff group, but users never see a login page and browsing fails. DNS to the Internet is only allowed by that same policy. What is the fix?",["Change the captive portal port to TCP 53","Disable the implicit deny policy so unmatched traffic can reach the portal","Allow DNS in a policy above it with no user requirement","Set the policy action to Monitor"],2,"Before the login page can appear, the browser must resolve names, and DNS cannot trigger the login prompt. A separate DNS policy without users solves it; disabling the implicit deny would be unsafe.","Configure different methods of firewall authentication",["Captive portal does not run on TCP 53; changing the portal port to 53 does not fix name resolution before login.","Disabling the implicit deny is unsafe and unnecessary; the real problem is DNS being gated behind the auth policy.",null,"Setting the policy to Monitor changes logging/action but still does not let DNS resolve before the login page appears."]],["fg36",0,2,"What is the difference between active and passive firewall authentication?",["Active uses RADIUS; passive uses only local users","Active prompts the user; passive identifies them from other sources","Active works only for admins; passive only for guests","Active needs FSSO; passive needs a captive portal"],1,"Active authentication asks the user for credentials (captive portal). Passive methods such as FSSO learn who is logged on from Windows logon events without a prompt.","Configure different methods of firewall authentication",["Active authentication is not tied to RADIUS, and passive is not limited to local users; the difference is prompting vs not.",null,"Active is not admin-only and passive is not guest-only; both apply to firewall users generally.","Active uses a captive portal (not FSSO), and passive methods like FSSO do not require a captive portal."]],["fg37",0,2,"The FortiGate must look up users' group memberships in Active Directory over LDAP. Which bind type should the LDAP server entry use?",["Anonymous, so no credentials are stored","Regular, with a service account's DN and password","Simple, with the admin's own account","Kerberos, with no bind account configured at all"],1,"Regular bind lets FortiGate log in with a service account to search the directory. Active Directory normally rejects anonymous searches, and simple bind cannot search group memberships.","Configure different methods of firewall authentication",["Active Directory normally rejects anonymous searches, so an anonymous bind cannot look up group memberships.",null,"Simple bind with the admin's own account is not how FortiGate searches AD; a regular service-account bind is used.","FortiGate LDAP does not use a Kerberos bind with no account; it needs a regular bind account to search."]],["fg38",0,2,"Only members of the AD group Sales should be able to use a policy. The LDAP server is already defined. What should the admin do next?",["Create a firewall user group referencing the LDAP server and Sales DN","Create a local user named Sales with LDAP as its password","Add the LDAP server directly to the policy source","Create an administrator profile named Sales"],0,"A firewall user group with a remote server and a group match lets policies require that AD group. Policies take user groups, not LDAP server entries, and admin profiles are for administrators.","Configure different methods of firewall authentication",[null,"A local user with LDAP as its password is not how remote AD groups are referenced; you use a user group.","Policies reference user groups, not raw LDAP server entries, so adding the server to the source will not work.","An administrator profile governs GUI admins, not firewall users authenticating to a policy."]],["fg39",0,2,"In FSSO DC agent mode, what does the DC agent do?",["Prompts users for credentials in their browser","Sends user logons straight to FortiAnalyzer","Polls each workstation's registry remotely for the logged-on user name","Captures DC logon events and sends them to the collector agent"],3,"The DC agent runs on each domain controller and forwards logon events to the collector agent, which updates the FortiGate. Browser prompts are active authentication.","Explain how to deploy and configure FSSO",["Browser credential prompts are active (captive portal) authentication, not what the FSSO DC agent does.","The DC agent sends logon events to the collector agent, not directly to FortiAnalyzer.","Remotely polling each workstation's registry describes a different (agentless workstation) method, not DC agent mode.",null]],["fg40",0,2,"The Windows team will not allow any software on the domain controllers, but the FortiGate must still identify users with FSSO. What should be used?",["DC agent mode with the agent on each member server","RADIUS single sign-on from the domain controllers","Polling mode, reading the DC security event logs remotely","Captive portal authentication with LDAP"],2,"Polling mode (from the collector agent or agentless on the FortiGate) reads logon events without software on the DCs. A captive portal is active, not single sign-on.","Explain how to deploy and configure FSSO",["DC agent mode requires software on the domain controllers, which the Windows team has forbidden.","RADIUS single sign-on is a different mechanism and is not the FSSO polling option that reads DC event logs.",null,"A captive portal is active authentication that prompts users; it is not FSSO single sign-on."]],["fg41",0,2,"FSSO users from most AD groups match policies, but members of the new Finance group never do. What is the most likely cause?",["Finance users log in more often than other users","The implicit deny policy is set to log all sessions","Finance is not in the groups monitored by the FSSO setup","The Finance group name contains uppercase letters that FSSO ignores"],2,"FSSO only sends groups that are selected (group filter) on the collector or FortiGate, so a new group must be added. Logging settings and name case do not stop matching.","Explain how to deploy and configure FSSO",["How often users log in does not affect FSSO matching; the group simply must be in the monitored set.","Implicit-deny logging records dropped traffic; it does not stop specific FSSO groups from matching policies.",null,"FSSO does not silently drop groups because of uppercase letters; the group must be added to the filter."]],["fg42",0,2,"The company wants two-factor authentication for firewall users with Fortinet's own tokens. What should the admin configure?",["Enable two-factor on the admin profile only","Assign a FortiToken to each user account","Add a second LDAP server as a backup","Require longer passwords on the local users"],1,"FortiToken (hardware or mobile) is assigned per user to add a one-time code. Admin profile settings affect admins, and a backup LDAP server is redundancy, not a second factor.","Configure different methods of firewall authentication",["Two-factor on the admin profile protects administrators, not the firewall users who need MFA here.",null,"A backup LDAP server adds redundancy for authentication, not a second authentication factor.","Longer passwords strengthen a single factor but do not provide the two-factor token the requirement asks for."]],["fg43",0,3,"What does certificate inspection look at in an HTTPS session?",["The TLS handshake details such as SNI and certificate names","The full decrypted HTTP request and response","Only the destination port number","Files downloaded inside the session, compared with antivirus signatures"],0,"Certificate inspection reads the unencrypted handshake (SNI and certificate) without decrypting the payload, so it cannot scan files. Deep inspection decrypts the content.","Inspect encrypted traffic using certificates",[null,"Reading the full decrypted request and response requires deep inspection, not certificate inspection.","Certificate inspection reads handshake details, not just the destination port number.","Scanning downloaded files against AV signatures requires decrypting the payload, which certificate inspection does not do."]],["fg44",0,3,"Antivirus catches test malware over HTTP but not over HTTPS. The policy uses certificate inspection. What should change?",["Switch the antivirus profile to quick scan","Enable certificate inspection on the WAN port too","Increase the antivirus maximum file size","Use deep inspection so the payload is decrypted"],3,"Without decryption the FortiGate cannot see files inside TLS. Deep inspection decrypts and re-encrypts so AV can scan them.","Inspect encrypted traffic using certificates",["A quick-scan AV setting does not let the FortiGate see inside encrypted TLS; the traffic still is not decrypted.","Enabling certificate inspection on the WAN port still does not decrypt payloads, so files inside HTTPS stay hidden.","Raising the AV maximum file size does not help when the file cannot be seen at all without decryption.",null]],["fg45",0,3,"After deep inspection is enabled, every user sees browser certificate warnings. What is the proper fix?",["Switch the profile back to certificate inspection only","Import each website's certificate into the FortiGate","Disable TLS 1.3 on the FortiGate","Trust the FortiGate's signing CA on all client devices"],3,"The FortiGate re-signs server certificates with its CA, so clients must trust that CA (pushed by GPO or MDM). Switching back removes decryption, and importing site certificates is not how it works.","Inspect encrypted traffic using certificates",["Reverting to certificate inspection removes decryption entirely, losing the deep inspection you wanted to keep.","You do not import each website's certificate; the FortiGate re-signs with its own CA, which clients must trust.","Disabling TLS 1.3 does not remove the warnings, which come from clients not trusting the FortiGate signing CA.",null]],["fg46",0,3,"Legal requires that online banking traffic is never decrypted. How can deep inspection stay in place for everything else?",["Put banking sites in a static URL filter with Block","Use a separate policy with no security profiles for everyone","Exempt the Finance and Banking category in the SSL profile","Disable SSL inspection on Fridays with a schedule"],2,"SSL inspection profiles can exempt FortiGuard categories or addresses from decryption. Blocking banking is wrong, and removing profiles for everyone loses protection.","Inspect encrypted traffic using certificates",["Blocking banking sites denies the traffic entirely; the goal is to allow it while never decrypting it.","Removing all security profiles for everyone loses protection broadly; you only need to exempt banking from decryption.",null,"A Friday schedule that disables SSL inspection is arbitrary and still decrypts banking on other days."]],["fg47",0,3,"A mobile banking app fails only when deep inspection is applied, though browsers work fine. What is the likely cause?",["The app requires certificate inspection to be off","The app pins its certificate and rejects the re-signed one","The FortiGate blocks every destination port above 1024","The app uses IPv6, which deep inspection always drops on FortiOS 7.6"],1,"Certificate pinning makes the app accept only its expected certificate, so it rejects the FortiGate's. Exempting the app's destinations from inspection fixes it.","Inspect encrypted traffic using certificates",["The problem is not that the app needs certificate inspection off generally; it is certificate pinning rejecting the re-signed cert.",null,"The FortiGate is not blocking high ports; browsers to the same service work, pointing to pinning rather than a port block.","FortiOS 7.6 does not universally drop IPv6 under deep inspection; the browser working rules this out, indicating pinning."]],["fg48",0,3,"How does proxy-based inspection compare with flow-based inspection?",["Proxy buffers content and offers more features at higher cost","Proxy is always faster because it skips buffering","Proxy only works with IPS, not antivirus","Proxy cannot scan HTTP traffic"],0,"Proxy mode buffers whole files and supports extra features, using more resources. Flow mode inspects as packets pass for lower latency.","Identify FortiGate inspection modes",[null,"Proxy mode buffers whole files, so it is generally slower than flow mode, not always faster.","Proxy inspection supports antivirus (including CDR), so it is not limited to IPS only.","Proxy mode can scan HTTP and other protocols; it is not unable to scan HTTP."]],["fg49",0,3,"In profile-based NGFW mode on FortiOS 7.6, where is the inspection mode (flow or proxy) chosen?",["In each firewall policy","Once globally for the whole FortiGate","In each antivirus profile only","On each physical interface"],0,"The inspection mode is set per firewall policy, so one policy can use proxy while another uses flow. It is not an interface or a global-only setting.","Identify FortiGate inspection modes",[null,"In profile-based mode the inspection mode is per policy, not a single global setting for the whole unit.","The flow/proxy choice is set on the policy, not inside the antivirus profile only.","Inspection mode is not chosen per physical interface; it is a per-policy setting."]],["fg50",0,3,"What is different in policy-based NGFW mode?",["Firewall policies are replaced by static routes","Security profiles can only use proxy inspection","Authentication is removed from the FortiGate","Applications and URL categories go directly into security policies"],3,"In policy-based mode, security policies reference applications and categories directly, while SSL inspection and address translation are handled in separate policies. Routing and authentication still exist.","Identify FortiGate inspection modes",["Static routes do not replace firewall policies in policy-based NGFW mode; routing still functions separately.","Security profiles are not restricted to proxy inspection in policy-based mode; flow inspection is still available.","Authentication is not removed in policy-based mode; user identification and auth still work.",null]],["fg51",0,3,"The Social Networking category is blocked, but the marketing team must reach one company page on a blocked site. The admin adds the URL to the static URL filter. Which action lets it through?",["Allow","Monitor","Exempt","Block"],2,"The static URL filter is checked before FortiGuard categories. Exempt skips the later checks, while Allow continues on to the category check, which would still block it.","Configure web filtering",["Allow permits the URL but still passes it to the FortiGuard category check, which would re-block the social-networking site.","Monitor only logs and permits without skipping later checks, so the category block would still apply.",null,"Block would deny the page, the opposite of letting the marketing team reach it."]],["fg52",0,3,"A FortiGuard category is set to Warning in the web filter profile. What does a user see?",["The site with a banner that cannot be closed","A warning page that lets them choose to continue","A login page that needs credentials from an allowed group","A block page with no way to proceed"],1,"Warning shows an interstitial page and lets the user proceed. Authenticate asks for credentials, and Block gives no choice.","Configure web filtering",["A non-closable banner is not the Warning behavior; Warning presents a page the user can click through.",null,"A credentials/login page is the Authenticate action, not Warning.","A block page with no way forward is the Block action, whereas Warning lets the user proceed."]],["fg53",0,3,"During a FortiGuard outage every website is blocked by the web filter. Which profile setting would let sites load when a rating cannot be obtained?",["Block invalid URLs","Allow websites when a rating error occurs","Enforce safe search on all search engines","Rate images by URL"],1,"When the rating server is unreachable, this option allows sites instead of blocking them. The other options affect invalid URLs, search engines and images.","Configure web filtering",["Blocking invalid URLs handles malformed addresses, not sites that fail because the rating server is unreachable.",null,"Enforcing safe search restricts search engines; it does nothing about rating-server outages.","Rating images by URL controls image classification, not what happens when a rating cannot be obtained."]],["fg54",0,3,"What is one advantage of a DNS filter over a web filter?",["It blocks bad domains at lookup time for any app, without decryption","It decrypts HTTPS without a CA certificate","It scans downloaded files for malware","It replaces the need for firewall policies"],0,"DNS filtering rates the domain in the query, so it works for any protocol and needs no TLS inspection. It does not scan files or replace policies.","Configure web filtering",[null,"DNS filtering does not decrypt HTTPS; it inspects the DNS query, and no decryption or CA is involved.","DNS filtering does not scan downloaded files for malware; that is antivirus.","DNS filtering supplements firewall policies; it does not replace the need for them."]],["fg55",0,3,"The company must stop peer-to-peer file sharing such as BitTorrent. What is the most effective control?",["A firewall policy blocking TCP port 6881 only","A web filter blocking the peer-to-peer file sharing category","A DoS policy with a low session limit","An application control sensor blocking the P2P category"],3,"P2P apps change ports and use encryption, so signature-based application control is the reliable way. Blocking one port or web category misses most traffic.","Configure application control",["Blocking only TCP 6881 fails because P2P apps hop to other ports, so most BitTorrent traffic slips through.","The peer-to-peer web filter category rates websites, missing the app traffic that does not rely on those sites.","A DoS session limit throttles floods, not identified P2P application traffic.",null]],["fg56",0,3,"Facebook must stay allowed, but Facebook games must be blocked. How should application control be configured?",["Block the whole Social.Media category","Add facebook.com to the static URL filter as Block","Add an override that blocks only the Facebook games signature","Block the games category in the DNS filter"],2,"Application overrides act on specific signatures, so games can be blocked while Facebook stays allowed. Blocking the category or domain would block Facebook itself.","Configure application control",["Blocking the whole Social.Media category would block Facebook itself, not just its games.","Adding facebook.com to the URL filter as Block denies all of Facebook, not only the games.",null,"The DNS filter has no games category to block, and it cannot distinguish games within Facebook."]],["fg57",0,3,"Application control detects the cloud storage app but cannot block file uploads within it. Only certificate inspection is enabled. Why?",["Application control never works on HTTPS traffic","The app is on the FortiGuard allowlist","The actions inside the app are hidden without deep inspection","Upload control needs a proxy-mode DNS filter"],2,"The SNI reveals which app is in use, but actions such as upload are inside the encrypted payload. Deep inspection exposes them to the signatures.","Configure application control",["Application control does work on HTTPS via the SNI; the limitation is seeing actions inside the encrypted payload.","The app is not on an allowlist; the real reason upload actions are invisible is the lack of deep inspection.",null,"Upload control does not require a proxy-mode DNS filter; it requires deep inspection to see in-app actions."]],["fg58",0,3,"How does proxy-based antivirus handle a downloaded file?",["It scans packets as they pass and never buffers any data","It buffers the whole file, scans it, then sends it on","It only checks the file name against a list","It forwards the file and scans a copy later"],1,"Proxy mode holds the full file until the scan finishes, which lets it block or replace it. Flow mode scans as data passes and holds only the final packet until the verdict.","Configure antivirus scanning modes",["Scanning packets as they pass without buffering describes flow-based, not proxy-based, antivirus.",null,"Proxy AV inspects file content, not just the file name against a list.","Proxy AV holds the file until the scan finishes; it does not forward first and scan a copy later."]],["fg59",0,3,"Office documents in email should have active content such as macros removed before delivery. Which feature does this?",["Content disarm and reconstruction in proxy-based antivirus","Flow-based antivirus with the default database","An IPS sensor with a critical-severity filter","A DNS filter blocking newly observed domains"],0,"Content disarm and reconstruction strips active content and rebuilds the file; it needs proxy-based inspection. IPS and DNS filtering do not rebuild files.","Configure antivirus scanning modes",[null,"Flow-based antivirus scans for known malware but does not strip macros and rebuild files; CDR does that.","An IPS sensor detects exploit patterns; it does not remove active content from Office documents.","A DNS filter blocks domains at lookup time; it cannot disarm macros inside email attachments."]],["fg60",0,3,"What does sending files to FortiSandbox (or FortiGate Cloud Sandbox) add to antivirus scanning?",["A faster signature database for known viruses","Decryption of HTTPS traffic without a CA certificate","Blocking of spam email by sender reputation","Behavior analysis of unknown files in a sandbox"],3,"Sandboxing runs unknown files to see what they do, catching new malware that signatures miss. It does not decrypt traffic or filter spam.","Configure antivirus scanning modes",["Sandboxing is not simply a faster signature database; it executes unknown files to observe behavior.","Sandboxing does not decrypt HTTPS; decryption is done by SSL deep inspection, not the sandbox.","Spam filtering by sender reputation is antispam, not what a sandbox provides.",null]],["fg61",0,3,"An admin wants to protect Windows servers in the DMZ from known exploits without enabling every IPS signature. What should they do?",["Enable all signatures with action pass","Apply antivirus in proxy mode to the DMZ","Create a DoS policy for the DMZ subnet","Filter the IPS sensor by server target and Windows OS"],3,"IPS sensor filters select signatures by target, OS, severity and protocol, keeping inspection relevant. Antivirus and DoS policies do not stop exploit attempts.","Configure IPS",["Enabling all signatures with action pass would inspect nothing meaningfully and blocks no exploits.","Proxy-mode antivirus scans for malware in files, not exploit attempts against server vulnerabilities.","A DoS policy limits floods and anomalies, not known exploit signatures targeting the servers.",null]],["fg62",0,3,"A public web server is hit by a SYN flood. Which FortiGate feature is designed to limit it before firewall policy lookup?",["An application control override for the web server","A web filter quota","A DoS policy with a tcp_syn_flood threshold","An IP pool with overload"],2,"DoS policies are checked early and apply anomaly thresholds such as SYN floods. Application control, web filtering and IP pools do not handle floods.","Configure IPS",["An application control override identifies apps; it is not the early anomaly control that limits SYN floods.","A web filter quota limits browsing time/volume, not TCP SYN flood rates.",null,"An overload IP pool is source NAT; it does nothing to limit a SYN flood."]],["fg63",0,3,"What does IPS fail-open do when enabled?",["Blocks all traffic until the IPS engine recovers","Lets traffic pass uninspected if the IPS engine is overloaded","Sends traffic to FortiSandbox when IPS fails","Stops logging to save memory"],1,"Fail-open favors availability by passing traffic when the IPS engine cannot keep up. With it disabled, traffic is dropped instead.","Configure IPS",["Fail-open does the opposite of blocking all traffic; it lets traffic pass when IPS is overloaded.",null,"Fail-open passes traffic uninspected; it does not divert traffic to FortiSandbox when IPS fails.","Fail-open concerns inspection under load, not logging; it does not stop logging to save memory."]],["fg64",0,3,"An IPS signature is blocking a legitimate internal application between two known hosts. What is the most targeted fix?",["Add an IP exemption for those hosts on that signature","Disable the whole IPS sensor on the policy","Change every signature in the sensor to monitor","Move the application's traffic to a proxy-mode policy"],0,"An IP exemption keeps the signature active for everyone else. Disabling the sensor or setting everything to monitor removes protection widely.","Configure IPS",[null,"Disabling the whole sensor removes IPS protection for all traffic on the policy, far broader than needed.","Setting every signature to monitor stops blocking across the board, weakening protection widely.","Moving traffic to a proxy-mode policy does not target the one false-positive signature and changes inspection unnecessarily."]],["fg65",0,3,"The SOC wants to stop infected hosts from contacting known command-and-control servers. Which IPS sensor option helps?",["Block outgoing connections to botnet sites","Block malicious URLs in the DNS filter only","Enable the Warning action for all categories","Set the sensor's severity filter to low"],0,"The IPS sensor's botnet C&C option uses FortiGuard's botnet database to block outgoing connections. Warning pages and severity filters do not target C&C traffic.","Configure IPS",[null,"A DNS filter blocks malicious domains at lookup, but the question asks for an IPS sensor option for C&C traffic.","The Warning action shows click-through pages for web categories; it does not block botnet C&C connections.","A low-severity filter selects which signatures apply; it does not specifically block command-and-control traffic."]],["fg66",0,4,"A FortiGate has two static default routes: one with distance 10 and one with distance 20. Which is used?",["Both, with traffic shared between them","The distance 20 route, as the higher value wins","Whichever was configured most recently","The distance 10 route; the other waits inactive"],3,"The lower administrative distance wins, so only the distance 10 route is in the active routing table. The other stays in the routing database and becomes active if the first is removed.","Configure static routes",["Traffic is not shared between the two; only the lower-distance route is installed as active.","The higher distance value loses; lower administrative distance wins, so distance 20 is not used.","Recency does not decide route selection; administrative distance does.",null]],["fg67",0,4,"Two static default routes have the same distance 10, with priorities 0 and 5. What happens?",["Only the priority 5 route is installed in the table","Traffic is shared evenly between them","Both are in the table; the priority 0 route is used","Neither is installed because of a conflict"],2,"Equal distance puts both routes in the active table, and lower priority is preferred. This keeps the backup route in the table, which also helps reverse path checks.","Configure static routes",["The priority 5 route is not the only one installed; both go in the table with equal distance.","Traffic is not shared evenly here; the lower priority value is preferred, not load-balanced.",null,"Equal distance does not cause a conflict that drops both routes; both are installed."]],["fg68",0,4,"Two static default routes have the same distance and the same priority. How does the FortiGate forward traffic?",["Only through the route with the lower gateway IP address","Equal-cost multipath, by default based on source IP","Round robin per packet across both","Only through the first interface in the list"],1,"Equal distance and priority create ECMP, which by default spreads sessions by source IP. Per-packet round robin is not the default and would break sessions.","Configure static routes",["The lower gateway IP is not the tie-breaker; equal distance and priority produce ECMP load sharing.",null,"Per-packet round robin is not the default and would break stateful sessions; ECMP defaults to per-source-IP.","Traffic is not pinned to the first interface listed; both are used via ECMP."]],["fg69",0,4,"When does a FortiGate check policy routes compared with the routing table?",["Only after a routing table lookup fails to find any match","Policy routes are checked before the routing table","Only for traffic from the FortiGate itself","Only when ECMP is enabled on the FortiGate"],1,"Policy routes are evaluated first; if one matches, it sets the path. Only when none matches does the routing table decide.","Configure static routes",["Policy routes are checked first, not only after a routing-table lookup fails.",null,"Policy routes apply to transit traffic too, not only to traffic from the FortiGate itself.","Policy routes are always evaluated first; they are not conditional on ECMP being enabled."]],["fg70",0,4,"Traffic arriving on port3 from 172.20.5.0/24 is dropped, and debug flow shows a reverse path check failure. What is the likely cause?",["No route back to 172.20.5.0/24 through port3","The firewall policy lacks NAT","The implicit deny policy has logging on","The source subnet is on the FortiGuard blocklist"],0,"The reverse path forwarding check drops packets when the FortiGate has no route back to the source via the incoming interface. Adding a route for that subnet via port3 fixes it.","Configure static routes",[null,"A missing NAT setting would cause a NAT/translation issue, not a reverse-path-forwarding drop.","Implicit-deny logging only records drops; it does not cause an RPF failure.","An RPF drop is about the missing return route, not the source being on a FortiGuard blocklist."]],["fg71",0,4,"Which command shows inactive static routes as well as active ones?",["get router info routing-table all","get system arp","diagnose sys session list with a filter on the route","get router info routing-table database"],3,"The routing table database includes routes that are not installed, such as those with a higher distance. routing-table all shows only active routes.","Configure static routes",["get router info routing-table all shows only active routes, not the inactive ones in the database.","get system arp shows the ARP table (IP-to-MAC), not inactive static routes.","A session list shows live sessions, not the routing database of inactive routes.",null]],["fg72",0,4,"A branch has two ISPs. The admin wants the primary default route removed if the ISP's gateway stops answering pings. What should be configured?",["A lower priority on the backup route","A blackhole route with distance 254","A link health monitor on the primary WAN","A DoS policy on the primary WAN interface"],2,"A link health monitor probes a target and removes the route when it fails, so the backup takes over. Priorities do not detect failure, and a blackhole route drops traffic.","Configure static routes",["A lower priority on the backup route does not detect the primary failing; it only sets preference among active routes.","A blackhole route drops traffic; it does not monitor the gateway or remove the primary route on failure.",null,"A DoS policy limits attack traffic; it does not probe the gateway or withdraw a route when it stops responding."]],["fg73",0,4,"Why add a blackhole route for private ranges on a site with IPsec tunnels?",["So tunnels come up faster after a reboot","So ECMP can balance across tunnels","So traffic is dropped, not sent to the Internet, if a tunnel is down","So FortiGuard updates use the tunnel"],2,"When a tunnel route disappears, traffic for private subnets would follow the default route to the ISP. A high-distance blackhole route drops it instead.","Configure static routes",["Blackhole routes do not speed up tunnel establishment after a reboot; they only drop traffic for their prefixes.","Blackhole routes drop traffic; they do not enable ECMP balancing across tunnels.",null,"Blackhole routes are not about steering FortiGuard update traffic through the tunnel."]],["fg74",0,4,"What does an SD-WAN performance SLA measure?",["The bandwidth used by each user and each application","Latency, jitter and packet loss to probe servers","The number of firewall policies per zone","The FortiGuard rating of each destination"],1,"Performance SLAs send probes (such as ping, HTTP or DNS) through each member and track latency, jitter and loss. SD-WAN rules use these results.","Configure SD-WAN",["Per-user and per-application bandwidth is measured by traffic shaping/monitoring, not by a performance SLA.",null,"The number of policies per zone is a configuration count, not something an SLA probe measures.","FortiGuard destination ratings come from web filtering, not from SD-WAN performance SLA probes."]],["fg75",0,4,"Voice traffic must always use whichever SD-WAN member currently has the lowest jitter. Which SD-WAN rule strategy fits?",["Best quality, measuring jitter","Lowest cost (SLA)","Maximize bandwidth (SLA)","Manual, with the MPLS link first"],0,"Best quality picks the member with the best value of the chosen measure. Lowest cost picks the cheapest member that meets an SLA, not the best one.","Configure SD-WAN",[null,"Lowest cost (SLA) picks the cheapest member meeting the SLA, not necessarily the one with the lowest jitter.","Maximize bandwidth (SLA) spreads traffic across compliant members; it does not always pick the lowest-jitter link.","Manual pins traffic to a chosen member regardless of live jitter measurements."]],["fg76",0,4,"Traffic should use cheap broadband while it meets the SLA and only move to MPLS when broadband fails the SLA. Which strategy fits?",["Best quality","Manual","Maximize bandwidth (SLA)","Lowest cost (SLA)"],3,"Lowest cost (SLA) uses the lowest-cost member that meets the SLA target. Best quality would move traffic whenever MPLS scores even slightly better.","Configure SD-WAN",["Best quality would switch to MPLS whenever it scores slightly better, defeating the goal of staying on cheap broadband.","Manual pins traffic to a chosen link and does not automatically fail over based on SLA compliance.","Maximize bandwidth (SLA) load-balances across members rather than preferring the cheapest one until it fails the SLA.",null]],["fg77",0,4,"After adding wan1 and wan2 to SD-WAN, the admin cannot select wan1 in a firewall policy. What should the policy reference?",["The loopback interface","A VIP for each WAN address used by the members","The HA heartbeat interface","The SD-WAN zone that contains the members"],3,"Once interfaces are SD-WAN members, policies reference the SD-WAN zone. Loopbacks, VIPs and HA ports do not replace the zone.","Configure SD-WAN",["A loopback interface is not what SD-WAN policies reference; they use the SD-WAN zone.","VIPs are destination NAT objects, not a replacement for referencing the SD-WAN zone in a policy.","The HA heartbeat interface is for clustering, not for steering user traffic in a firewall policy.",null]],["fg78",0,4,"SD-WAN members, SLA and rules are configured, but Internet traffic still fails. The old static default routes on wan1 and wan2 were deleted. What is missing?",["A policy route for every user subnet in the LAN","A second performance SLA","A static default route using the SD-WAN zone","An IP pool on the SD-WAN zone"],2,"SD-WAN rules only steer traffic that the routing table already sends to SD-WAN, so a route pointing at the zone is needed. Extra SLAs or IP pools do not add a route.","Configure SD-WAN",["Per-subnet policy routes are not the missing piece; SD-WAN still needs a route sending traffic to the zone.","A second performance SLA does not add a route; the routing table still has no path to the SD-WAN zone.",null,"An IP pool provides source NAT addresses; it does not create the default route SD-WAN requires."]],["fg79",0,5,"Which ports must be open between two IPsec peers when one of them is behind a NAT device?",["TCP 443 and TCP 8443","UDP 500 and UDP 4500","UDP 1701 and TCP 1723","TCP 500 and TCP 4500"],1,"IKE uses UDP 500, and NAT traversal moves traffic to UDP 4500. L2TP and PPTP use 1701 and 1723.","Implement IPsec VPN",["TCP 443/8443 are HTTPS/SSL VPN ports, not the IKE and NAT-T ports IPsec uses.",null,"UDP 1701 and TCP 1723 are L2TP and PPTP, not IPsec IKE/NAT-T.","IKE and NAT-T use UDP 500 and UDP 4500, not TCP 500/4500."]],["fg80",0,5,"A site-to-site tunnel's phase 1 comes up, but phase 2 never does. What is the most likely cause?",["Phase 2 selectors or proposals do not match","The pre-shared key is wrong on one of the peers","UDP 500 is blocked by the ISP","The peers use different IKE versions"],0,"Phase 1 success proves the key, IKE version and reachability are fine. Phase 2 fails when subnets (selectors) or encryption proposals differ between peers.","Implement IPsec VPN",[null,"A wrong pre-shared key would fail phase 1; since phase 1 is up, the key is already proven correct.","If UDP 500 were blocked, phase 1 could not complete either; but phase 1 is up, so it is not blocked.","Mismatched IKE versions would prevent phase 1 from forming; phase 1 succeeding rules this out."]],["fg81",0,5,"Why is a route-based (interface-mode) IPsec VPN usually preferred over a policy-based one?",["It creates a tunnel interface usable in routes and policies","It needs no firewall policies at all","It encrypts traffic with stronger algorithms","It can only be built with IKEv1"],0,"Route-based VPNs create a virtual interface, so routes (including dynamic routing and backup routes) and normal policies can use it. Encryption strength is the same in both.","Implement IPsec VPN",[null,"Route-based VPNs still need firewall policies for the tunnel traffic; they do not eliminate policies.","Encryption strength is identical between route-based and policy-based VPNs; that is not the advantage.","Route-based VPNs support IKEv1 and IKEv2; they are not limited to IKEv1."]],["fg82",0,5,"A new route-based tunnel shows as up, but no traffic passes between the two LANs. What is most likely missing?",["A second phase 1 with aggressive mode","A VIP for each remote host","NAT-T disabled on both peers","A route via the tunnel and policies between LAN and tunnel"],3,"An up tunnel still needs a route to the remote subnet through the tunnel interface and firewall policies in both directions. VIPs and NAT-T changes are not required.","Implement IPsec VPN",["A second phase 1 in aggressive mode is not needed; the tunnel is up but lacks a route and policies.","A VIP per remote host is destination NAT and is not what a route-based tunnel needs to pass LAN traffic.","Disabling NAT-T is not required; the missing pieces are the route via the tunnel and the policies.",null]],["fg83",0,5,"Headquarters has two ISPs and wants branch tunnels to fail over automatically. Which design fits?",["One policy-based tunnel with two phase 2 selectors","Two tunnels with identical routes and DPD turned off","Two route-based tunnels with different route distances and DPD","One tunnel whose peer is changed by hand"],2,"Two tunnels (one per ISP) with preferred and backup routes give failover, and DPD detects the dead tunnel so its route is withdrawn. Without DPD the dead tunnel may stay up.","Implement a meshed or partially redundant IPsec VPN",["A single policy-based tunnel with two selectors does not provide ISP-level failover across two paths.","Identical routes with DPD off give no clear preferred/backup path and cannot detect a dead tunnel to fail over.",null,"Manually changing the peer is not automatic failover; it requires human intervention on each outage."]],["fg84",0,5,"What does dead peer detection (DPD) do?",["Encrypts traffic twice to stop replay attacks from outside peers","Detects an unresponsive peer so the tunnel is torn down","Blocks peers found on FortiGuard blocklists","Renews the phase 2 key after every packet"],1,"DPD sends probes and brings the tunnel down when the peer stops answering, so routes can move to a backup. It does not affect encryption.","Implement a meshed or partially redundant IPsec VPN",["Encrypting traffic twice is not what DPD does; it detects unresponsive peers to tear down the tunnel.",null,"DPD does not consult FortiGuard blocklists; it probes the configured peer for responsiveness.","DPD does not rekey phase 2 after every packet; PFS/rekey timers handle key renewal separately."]],["fg85",0,5,"Five sites must be connected in a full mesh of IPsec tunnels. How many tunnels are needed?",["5","10","20","25"],1,"A full mesh needs n(n−1)/2 tunnels, and 5×4/2 = 10. Hub and spoke would need only 4.","Implement a meshed or partially redundant IPsec VPN",["5 would be the count only if each site needed one tunnel; a full mesh needs n(n-1)/2, which is 10.",null,"20 double-counts the tunnels; each pair shares one tunnel, giving 10, not 20.","25 is 5 squared and counts nonexistent self-pairs; the correct full-mesh total is 10."]],["fg86",0,5,"In a hub-and-spoke VPN, spokes should build direct tunnels to each other on demand instead of sending all traffic through the hub. What should be used?",["ADVPN","A full mesh of static tunnels","Policy-based VPN on the hub","SSL VPN web mode"],0,"ADVPN lets spokes create short-cut tunnels on demand. A full static mesh works but must be built and maintained by hand.","Implement a meshed or partially redundant IPsec VPN",[null,"A full static mesh gives direct paths but must be built and maintained by hand, unlike on-demand ADVPN.","A policy-based VPN on the hub does not create on-demand spoke-to-spoke shortcut tunnels.","SSL VPN web mode is remote user access to resources, not dynamic spoke-to-spoke IPsec shortcuts."]],["fg87",0,5,"A branch gets a dynamic public IP from its ISP, while headquarters has a static IP. How should phase 1 be set up?",["Both use static peers with each other's current IPs","Both use dial-up peers so either side can start the tunnel","The branch uses dial-up and HQ uses static","HQ uses a dial-up (dynamic) peer; the branch points to HQ"],3,"The side with an unknown address must start the tunnel, so HQ accepts it as a dial-up peer and the branch uses HQ's static IP. Two dial-up peers could never reach each other.","Implement IPsec VPN",["The branch's IP is unknown to HQ, so HQ cannot be configured with the branch's current static peer address.","Two dial-up peers both wait to be contacted and would never initiate, so the tunnel could never form.","This reverses the roles: the dynamic-IP branch must initiate, and HQ must accept it as the dial-up peer.",null]],["fg88",0,5,"Which commands show IKE negotiation messages in real time?",["get vpn ipsec stats crypto, then execute ping","diagnose sys session list, then diagnose debug reset","diagnose debug application ike -1, then diagnose debug enable","get router info routing-table all, then diagnose debug disable"],2,"The IKE debug with level -1 and debug output enabled shows negotiation steps. Session lists and routing tables do not show IKE messages.","Implement IPsec VPN",["get vpn ipsec stats crypto shows crypto counters, not the live IKE negotiation messages.","A session list and debug reset do not display IKE negotiation steps in real time.",null,"The routing table and disabling debug do not show IKE negotiation messages."]],["fg89",0,5,"What does enabling perfect forward secrecy (PFS) in phase 2 do?",["Uses the phase 1 key for all phase 2 keys","Sends traffic through the tunnel unencrypted as a connectivity test","Runs a new Diffie-Hellman exchange for each phase 2 key","Lets peers skip phase 1 on reconnect"],2,"PFS generates fresh key material, so a compromise of one key does not expose others. Without PFS, phase 2 keys come from phase 1 material.","Implement IPsec VPN",["Reusing the phase 1 key for all phase 2 keys is what happens without PFS, the opposite of enabling it.","PFS does not send traffic unencrypted; it strengthens keying, and the tunnel stays encrypted.",null,"PFS does not let peers skip phase 1 on reconnect; it adds a fresh DH exchange for phase 2 keys."]],["fg90",0,5,"Which command lists the current IKE (phase 1) gateways and their status?",["get system interface physical","diagnose vpn ike gateway list","diagnose sys ha status","get router info bgp summary"],1,"diagnose vpn ike gateway list shows each phase 1 gateway, its state and negotiated settings. The others show interfaces, HA and BGP.","Implement IPsec VPN",["get system interface physical shows physical interface details, not IKE phase 1 gateway status.",null,"diagnose sys ha status reports HA cluster state, not IKE gateways.","get router info bgp summary shows BGP peers, not IPsec IKE phase 1 gateways."]]]);
